        "payload": "tree e7852146f9e49beb782a0a9387b9733033b250c5\nparent 9183edd25e60aa410bc3e4728989a1d75c309a09\nauthor Jameson Lopp <jameson.lopp@gmail.com> 1794592302 -0500\ncommitter Jameson Lopp <jameson.lopp@gmail.com> 1794666748 -0500\n\nConfiguration argument handling improvements.\n\n- Several numeric configuration options now reject values that are out of\n  range instead of silently wrapping or inverting. Previously a negative value\n  could disable the intended limit entirely.\n\n  - `-dbbatchsize` and `-dbcrashratio` must be non-negative. A negative\n    `-dbbatchsize` wrapped to a huge value and disabled batch flushing, so a\n    reindex could hold an unbounded batch in memory.\n  - `-limitclustersize`, `-limitancestorcount`, `-limitdescendantcount`,\n    `-mempoolexpiry`, `-datacarriersize`, `-maxsendbuffer`, `-maxreceivebuffer`,\n    `-bantime`, `-bytespersigop` and `-maxtipage` must be non-negative.\n  - `-limitclustercount` must be between 1 and 64.\n  - `-timeout` must be positive. It previously fell back to the default\n    silently, unlike the analogous `-peertimeout`, which already errored.\n\n  Zero remains valid wherever it already meant \"disabled\": `-dbbatchsize=0`,\n  `-limitclustersize=0`, `-datacarriersize=0`, `-mempoolexpiry=0`,\n  `-maxtipage=0`, `-bytespersigop=0`, `-maxsendbuffer=0`,\n  `-maxreceivebuffer=0` and `-bantime=0`.\n\n- The `-help` output for `-bytespersigop`, `-datacarriersize`, `-dbbatchsize`,\n  `-stopatheight` and `-checkblockindex` now shows the `=<n>` placeholder for\n  their value, matching how they are actually parsed.\n",
